\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for this test is approximately\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that $σ_{x}^{2} < σ_{y}^{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186172515 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 35$ and $n_{y} = 30$ from two normal populations having population variances $σ_{x}^{2}$ and $σ_{y}^{2}$, respectively. Unbiased estimates of the population variances obtained from the two samples are $s_{x}^{2} = 9^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 15^{2}$.\n\nWe wish to carry out a hypothesis test at a $2 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to test the hypothesis $H_{0}: σ_{x}^{2} = σ_{y}^{2}$ against the alternative $H_{1}: σ_{x}^{2} \\ne σ_{y}^{2}$.\n\nThe table below gives some values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W > w) = p$, where $W∼F(ν_{1}, ν_{2})$.\n\n| $ν_{2}∖ν_{1}$ | $28$ | $29$ | $30$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $33$ | $p = 0.020$<br>$p = 0.010$<br>$p = 0.005$ | $2.12$<br>$2.34$<br>$2.57$ | $2.11$<br>$2.33$<br>$2.56$ | $2.10$<br>$2.32$<br>$2.54$ |\n| $34$ | $p = 0.020$<br>$p = 0.010$<br>$p = 0.005$ | $2.10$<br>$2.32$<br>$2.55$ | $2.09$<br>$2.31$<br>$2.53$ | $2.08$<br>$2.30$<br>$2.52$ |\n| $35$ | $p = 0.020$<br>$p = 0.010$<br>$p = 0.005$ | $2.09$<br>$2.30$<br>$2.53$ | $2.08$<br>$2.29$<br>$2.51$ | $2.07$<br>$2.28$<br>$2.50$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for this test is approximately\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $2 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that $σ_{x}^{2} \\ne σ_{y}^{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186172516 :question/problem "A particular feature is being examined across $4$ groups of individuals to determine if the mean varies by group. The summary statistics of three samples, one from each group, are given in the table below, where $n_{k}$ is the size of each sample, and $s_{k}$ is the sample standard deviation.\n\n|  | Sample 1 | Sample 2 | Sample 3 | Sample 4 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $n_{k}$ | $12$ | $8$ | $11$ | $10$ |\n| $s_{k}$ | $2.4$ | $1.8$ | $2.1$ | $1.6$ |\n\nWhat is the sum of squares within groups (SSW) of these samples?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186172517 :question/problem "A particular feature is being examined across $3$ groups of individuals to determine if the mean varies by group. The table below shows the values of this feature for three samples. The first sample contains $3$ individuals from the first group, the second sample contains $5$ individuals from the second group, and the third sample contains $4$ individuals from the third group.\n\n| Group 1 | Group 2 | Group 3 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $7.8$ | $7.5$ | $8.7$ |\n| $8.2$ | $9$ | $10.3$ |\n| $5$ | $10.5$ | $11$ |\n|  | $8.9$ | $12$ |\n|  | $9.1$ |  |\n\nWhat is the sum of squares within groups (SSW) for these samples?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186172518 :question/problem "A particular feature is being examined across $3$ groups of individuals to determine if the mean varies by group. The table below shows the values of this feature for three samples. The first sample contains $4$ individuals from the first group, the second sample contains $3$ individuals from the second group, and the third sample contains $5$ individuals from the third group.\n\n| Group 1 | Group 2 | Group 3 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $8.2$ | $10.3$ | $4.9$ |\n| $6.8$ | $9.4$ | $6.3$ |\n| $6$ | $9.1$ | $8.6$ |\n| $6.2$ |  | $6.5$ |\n|  |  | $7.7$ |\n\nGiven that the grand sample mean is $7.5$, what is the sum of squares between groups (SSB) of these samples?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186172521 :question/problem "Two random variables, $A$ and $